      "feedback": "Here is an analysis and review of your draft preview page for Devil's Backbone / Slipper Spurge (Euphorbia tithymaloides) [PSBP-00572]:\n\n1. Fact-Checking & Critical Errors\nNectar Flag Mismatch: The index flag box lists Nectar — no. However, the Wildlife Value and More Information sections correctly state that the red shoe-shaped bracts produce nectar to attract hummingbirds. Since this is an important nectar source for hummingbirds, the index flag should be updated to Nectar ✓ yes.\n\nRing Species Terminology & Nuance: The text states it is \"the only known example of a ring species in the plant kingdom.\" While Cacho & Baum (2012) demonstrated ring speciation in Euphorbia tithymaloides, claiming it is the only one might be slightly over-bold ( botanists continue to evaluate candidates like Acacia karroo or certain Encelia complexes). Changing to \"the first clearly documented ring species in the plant kingdom\" keeps the huge hook while being scientifically airtight.\n\nSafety Callout Promine",
